//? Title: Bloom Filters: Exact Rejection, Approximate Membership
//? Subtitle: Visible probes, packed storage, the original load experiment and limits of the probability model
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Reject absent keys cheaply while retaining a compact summary of a set. A Bloom
filter stores probe bits rather than original keys: a clear queried bit proves
absence, while an all-set query requires an exact backing-set check. We inspect
a sixteen-bit example, retain the original 8,000-bit load experiment, and compare
measured false positives with the usual sizing approximation. The core now packs
its bits, exposes its actual probes and uses the standard FNV-1a string basis.
The probability formula is a model; the no-false-negative property follows
directly from the insert/query operations.
)

\toc

> The method

For a uint64 key x, compute a mixed base h1 and odd step h2, then use k positions.
The implementation's precise arithmetic is

$$
p_i=\big((h_1+i h_2)\bmod 2^{64}\big)\bmod m,
\qquad i=0,\ldots,k-1.
$$
\alttext(Each probe wraps the base plus i times the step modulo two to the sixty-fourth power, then reduces it modulo the logical bit count m.)

Insertion sets those bits. Query uses the same mixer and position function and
returns false when any probed bit is clear. Since later insertions only set bits,
an inserted key continues to have every probe set. This proves no false negatives
for a correctly used, insertion-only instance, independently of the quality or
independence of the hashes. Duplicate insertions are idempotent; there is no erase.

The two deterministic mixes use the SplitMix64 transformation. An odd step does
not guarantee distinct probes for every m, and uint64 wrapping before reduction
matters when m does not divide 2^64. Inspection uses the same probe calculation
as insertion and query. Positive membership is evidence to consult the exact
set, rather than a guarantee that the original item was inserted.

> Simple example: five set bits, one false positive

With m=16 and k=3, insert integer keys 10 and 20. Their probe lists are
[10,1,8] and [12,11,10]. The union has five set bits. Uninserted key 22 probes
[10,11,12], all already set, so it is a concrete false positive. Key 0 probes
[15,4,9], including clear bits, so it is correctly rejected.

```{cpp, file="figures/bloom_bits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::BloomFilter filter(16,3);filter.add(10);filter.add(20);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle bit;bit.width=.52;bit.height=.6;bit.font_size=16;bit.padding=4;
  for (int p=0;p<filter.num_bits();++p) {
    bit.fill=filter.bit_is_set(p)?dm::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::RGB{248,250,252};
    diagram.node(std::format("b{}",p),{.65*p,0},filter.bit_is_set(p)?"1":"0",bit)
        .text({.65*p,.75},std::to_string(p),16);
  }
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle query;query.width=4.5;query.height=1.2;query.fill={255,237,213};
  diagram.node("query",{6.6,-2.7},std::format("Key 22 was not inserted\nProbes {}: maybe present",filter.probe_positions(22)),query);
  for (int p:filter.probe_positions(22)) diagram.edge("query",std::format("b{}",p));
  diagram.text({4.875,1.8},"16 logical bits after inserting integer keys 10 and 20")
      .text({1.9,-1.6},std::format("Key 10: {}",filter.probe_positions(10)),16)
      .text({1.9,-2.5},std::format("Key 20: {}",filter.probe_positions(20)),16);
  diagram.save_svg("build/bloom_bits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/bloom_bits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Every logical bit and arrow address comes from the core inspection API. Blue cells are set; key 22's three arrows all reach blue cells despite its absence from the inserted set.)
\alttext(Sixteen cells show ones at positions one, eight, ten, eleven and twelve. A query for uninserted key twenty-two points to positions ten, eleven and twelve and returns possibly present.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::algorithms::BloomFilter filter(16,3);filter.add(10);filter.add(20);
  std::print("Logical set bits: {}; fill: {:.4f}; packed payload: {} bytes.\n\n",
             filter.num_set_bits(),filter.fill_ratio(),filter.storage_bytes());
  std::print("| integer key | actually inserted | probe positions | probe bit values | query result |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t key:{10,20,22,0}) {
    const auto positions=filter.probe_positions(key);std::vector<int> values;
    for (int p:positions) values.push_back(filter.bit_is_set(p));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",key,key==10||key==20,positions,values,filter.maybe_contains(key));
  }
}
```
