\caption(The model informs sizing, while the deterministic experiment shows what this workload does. Correlated and repeated probes prevent interpreting the model as an exact guarantee.)
\alttext(The model error decreases from one probe toward six probes and then rises. Separate observed points show the finite deterministic workload.)

> A finite counterexample to an exact probability claim

One logical bit is enough to expose the distinction. After inserting any key,
every probe of every absent key addresses that same set bit, so the false-positive
probability is one. The independent-hash approximation is below one for finite
n=1. Repeated insertion of that same key changes neither the state nor the load.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| one-bit probe count | set bits after 100 copies of key 42 | absent key 99 tests positive | model at one distinct key |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int k:{1,3,10}) {
    dm::algorithms::BloomFilter filter(1,k);
    for (int i=0;i<100;++i) filter.add(42);
    if (filter.num_set_bits()!=1 || !filter.maybe_contains(99)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",k,filter.num_set_bits(),filter.maybe_contains(99),filter.expected_false_positive_rate(1));
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| one-bit probe count | set bits after 100 copies of key 42 | absent key 99 tests positive | model at one distinct key |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 1 | 1 | true | 0.632121 |
| 3 | 1 | true | 0.857952 |
| 10 | 1 | true | 0.999546 |
// result_end
\caption(These deliberately tiny filters retain the deterministic no-false-negative property while invalidating an exact reading of the approximate false-positive formula.)

> Conclusion

The sixteen-bit example separates exact rejection from a concrete false positive.
The original experiment now shows current, standards-based string hashes and
actual packed storage. The load and hash-count sweeps test the model's role as
a sizing approximation, while the one-bit counterexample states its limit.

Insert and query cost O(k), with early rejection for a clear query bit. Set-bit
count and fill are O(1), maintained on zero-to-one transitions. The packed payload
uses \(64\lceil m/64\rceil\) bits, plus object/allocation overhead, rather
than the previous byte per logical bit. Dimensions and queried bit coordinates
are checked. Native tests compare packed words with a boolean-array oracle across
word boundaries, duplicates, copies and saturation, and verify official FNV vectors.

No observed dataset is required for these set invariants. Deterministic hashes
and the approximate probability model do not provide cryptographic protection,
deletion support, an exact membership answer or a workload-independent error
guarantee. Exact verification remains the consumer's responsibility after a
positive result.

> References

- Bloom, B. H. (1970). *Space/time trade-offs in hash coding with allowable errors*. Communications of the ACM, 13(7), 422–426. https://doi.org/10.1145/362686.362692.
- Kirsch, A. and Mitzenmacher, M. (2008). *Less hashing, same performance: Building a better Bloom filter*. Random Structures & Algorithms, 33(2), 187–218. https://doi.org/10.1002/rsa.20208. The finite wrapped arithmetic here is stated explicitly above.
- Noll, L., Vo, K., Eastlake, D. and Hansen, T. (2026). *The FNV Non-Cryptographic Hash Algorithm*. RFC 9923. https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9923.html.
- Vigna, S. (2015). Public-domain SplitMix64 implementation. https://prng.di.unimi.it/splitmix64.c. The stateless output transformation supplies the two mixed values here.
